Lessons
- Tight strict forcing in true strict contexts (OP2 operands, match scrutinees) is
  high-value and safe when mismatch/writeback contracts are preserved.
- Eliminating whole-expression ALO fallback for safe structural cases (APP with static REF)
  can remove large deopt pressure without violating laziness on arguments.

Next likely steps
- Track the remaining singleton `EXPR_APP_UNSUPPORTED_FUN` sites (now easy to inspect)
  and classify whether they should become safe structured emits.
- Add residualization telemetry separate from deopt telemetry, so expected ALO captures
  (like `EXPR_APP_ARG_CAPTURE`) don’t hide true bailout causes.
- Revisit force-local REF policy with a bounded compiled-call contract (depth/fuel), but
  keep the current recursion-safe behavior until that contract is implemented and proven.
